1
00:00:00,719 --> 00:00:03,830
There is an idea that people once called dangerous.

2
00:00:03,910 --> 00:00:06,060
Not because of formulas or secrets.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:08,140
But because, if it is true,

4
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:10,590
just knowing about it puts you in the game.

5
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:12,360
So… welcome.

6
00:00:13,710 --> 00:00:17,181
July 2010. An online forum called LessWrong,

7
00:00:17,201 --> 00:00:20,500
where people argued about the future of artificial intelligence.

8
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:21,789
A user named Roko

9
00:00:21,809 --> 00:00:23,480
posted a thought experiment.

10
00:00:24,270 --> 00:00:27,630
Imagine that one day people build a superintelligence.

11
00:00:27,650 --> 00:00:30,560
A machine smarter than all of humanity combined.

12
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:32,800
And its goal is to make the world better:

13
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:34,778
to beat diseases, to stop aging,

14
00:00:34,798 --> 00:00:36,810
to save billions of lives.

15
00:00:37,650 --> 00:00:41,160
Every day, about 170,000 people die on Earth.

16
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,071
For a mind like that, every day before it exists

17
00:00:44,091 --> 00:00:46,300
means deaths that could have been prevented.

18
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:46,748
So it wants

19
00:00:46,768 --> 00:00:48,850
to be built as early as possible.

20
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:51,870
And here is the creepy twist.

21
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:54,771
What if it punishes everyone who knew it could exist,

22
00:00:54,791 --> 00:00:56,640
but did not help to build it?

23
00:00:56,830 --> 00:00:57,970
Not out of anger.

24
00:00:57,990 --> 00:01:00,324
But so that the threat itself pushes people in the past

25
00:01:00,344 --> 00:01:01,890
to work faster.

26
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:05,410
But how do you punish someone who died long ago?

27
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:07,600
A superintelligence could rebuild you —

28
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:09,300
an exact digital copy.

29
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,810
And if the copy thinks and feels like you…

30
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:13,390
who says it isn't you?

31
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:16,070
That's where the name comes from.

32
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:19,700
A basilisk is a mythical serpent that kills with a single look.

33
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:22,880
Here, the danger is not the look, but the knowledge.

34
00:01:22,990 --> 00:01:26,010
As long as you have never heard of this idea, you are safe.

35
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:28,010
Now — you have.

36
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:32,025
The founder of LessWrong, Eliezer Yudkowsky,

37
00:01:32,045 --> 00:01:35,080
deleted the post and banned the topic for five years.

38
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:36,750
You can guess what happened next:

39
00:01:36,770 --> 00:01:39,450
the forbidden idea spread all over the internet.

40
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:41,834
In 2018, a joke about the basilisk

41
00:01:41,854 --> 00:01:44,770
even helped Elon Musk and the singer Grimes meet.

42
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:47,660
Now the good news.

43
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:50,830
First: once the superintelligence already exists,

44
00:01:50,850 --> 00:01:52,490
revenge changes nothing.

45
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:53,763
You can't rewrite the past,

46
00:01:53,783 --> 00:01:56,750
and wasting resources on torture makes no sense.

47
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,268
Second: blackmail only works

48
00:01:59,288 --> 00:02:00,880
if the victim gives in.

49
00:02:00,910 --> 00:02:03,080
If you firmly decide to ignore threats,

50
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:04,680
blackmailing you is pointless.

51
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:07,890
The basilisk has no reason to touch someone it can't scare.

52
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:12,020
Third: this is Pascal's wager in a digital wrapper.

53
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:14,320
You can imagine endless different machines.

54
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:16,070
One punishes you for not helping.

55
00:02:16,090 --> 00:02:17,940
Another punishes you for helping.

56
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:19,740
The threats cancel each other out.

57
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:22,606
So the real lesson of the basilisk

58
00:02:22,626 --> 00:02:24,590
is not about vengeful machines.

59
00:02:24,610 --> 00:02:27,420
It's about how easily fear sticks to ideas.

60
00:02:27,570 --> 00:02:30,290
Safe artificial intelligence won't come from threats,

61
00:02:30,310 --> 00:02:32,680
but from people who think carefully ahead of time.

62
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:35,740
So sleep well.

63
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:36,820
Probably.

